Task 4.4: Prevent Recurrence (1 Mark) 
• Rule 1 — Always Manage Files with 'with' and Explicit Encoding: Never call bare open() without a with 
statement and explicit encoding='utf-8'; this guarantees deterministic file closure and buffer flushing even 
when exceptions occur. 
• Rule 2 — Choose File Modes Deliberately and Persist Diagnostics: Verify whether cumulative logs require 
append mode ('a') rather than destructive write mode ('w'), preserve explicit '\n' line delimiters on writes, and 
route all operational exceptions to a persistent error log rather than ephemeral console prints. 
Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis (Task 4) 
Alternative Solution: Opening files manually and closing them inside a 'finally:' block (e.g., f = open(...); try: ... finally: 
f.close()). 
• Time & Space Complexity: Identical to 'with' statements—O(n) time to read/write n characters and O(1) auxiliary 
descriptor overhead—because Python's context manager protocol (__enter__/__exit__) compiles down to the exact 
same try/finally cleanup semantics. 
• Open Files on Error: Properly written nested try/finally blocks leave 0 files open on error, matching 'with'. 
However, if two files are opened sequentially before a single try/finally without nesting, an exception during the 
second open() skips the finally block and leaves 1 file open—making 'with' strictly safer and less error-prone. 
 
  
