Import Statement Comparison: 
• import attendance.stats: Loads the package and submodule, binding the full dotted name in the caller's 
namespace. Functions must be called as attendance.stats.percentage(...). Lowest risk of name collision. 
• from attendance import stats: Binds the submodule object 'stats' directly in the local namespace. Functions 
are called as stats.percentage(...). Balances brevity with clear origin. 
• from attendance.stats import percentage: Binds only the function object 'percentage' directly into the caller's 
local namespace. Called simply as percentage(...), which is concise but can shadow local functions with the 
same name. 
Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis (Task 5) 
Alternative Solution: Keeping all functions inside a single monolithic script rather than splitting into a multi-file 
package. 
• One-Time Import Cost & Memory: Importing a package incurs a one-time disk lookup and bytecode 
compilation/load cost across __init__.py and its submodules, storing module objects in sys.modules (~10–30 KB total 
overhead). Subsequent imports in the same process are O(1) dictionary lookups in sys.modules with zero extra I/O. 
• Maintenance & Lookup Effort: In a small script (<200 lines), locating a function is instant (Ctrl+F). Once a codebase 
reaches thousands of lines, a monolithic script forces developers to scan unrelated I/O, math, and formatting code 
together and risks unintended coupling. A package partitions the search space by domain (reader, stats, report), 
cutting cognitive load and preventing merge conflicts in team development. 
