Alternative Concept & Complexity Analysis (Task 6) 
Alternative Solution: Checking for duplicates by scanning a Python list of all previously accepted registrations ('if 
reg_no in [r["reg_no"] for r in master_list]:') instead of maintaining a hash set (seen_reg_nos). 
• Time Complexity for n Registrations: Linear list scanning performs 1 + 2 + ... + (n − 1) = n(n − 1)/2 comparisons, 
resulting in O(n²) quadratic time (e.g., ~12.5 million string comparisons for 5,000 students). A Python set uses open
addressed hash tables, delivering O(1) average lookup per record and O(n) total linear time. 
• Space Complexity: Scanning master_list directly uses O(n) space for the records alone with zero extra index 
memory. Maintaining a separate set of n register number strings uses O(n) additional hash-table memory (roughly 
32–64 bytes per entry plus string pointers, ~300 KB for 5,000 students)—a negligible memory cost in exchange for 
eliminating an O(n²) bottleneck. 
